> Simple examples

Training points 0 and 2 have targets 0 and 10. At query .5, uniform k=2 predicts 5; inverse-distance weights 2 and 2/3 predict 2.5. At a duplicate exact match, only zero-distance rows contribute.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nearest_neighbors_weights.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto dtype = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto references = n::tensor({0, 2}, {2, 1}, dtype),
             targets = n::tensor({0, 10}, {2}, dtype);
  const auto query = n::tensor({0, .5, 1, 1.5, 2}, {5, 1}, dtype);
  const n::ml::TensorNeighbors uniform(references, query, 2),
      weighted(references, query, 2, true);
  const auto a = uniform.regress(targets), b = weighted.regress(targets);
  if (n::max(n::abs(b - n::tensor({0, 2.5, 5, 7.5, 10}, {5}, dtype))).item() > 1e-10)
    return 1;
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.lines(query.to_vector<double>(), a.to_vector<double>(), "uniform", {37, 99, 235})
      .lines(query.to_vector<double>(), b.to_vector<double>(), "weighted", {234, 88, 12})
      .points({0, 2}, {0, 10}, "reference inputs", {22, 163, 74}, 6)
      .title("Tensor distances and weighted gathered targets")
      .x_label("query x")
      .y_label("prediction");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.y_limits(-1, 13).size(850, 420).save_svg("build/nearest_neighbors_weights.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  t.add_column("x", query.to_vector<double>());
  t.add_column("uniform", a.to_vector<double>());
  t.add_column("weighted", b.to_vector<double>());
  std::print("{}\n", t.to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| x | uniform | weighted |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.0 | 5 | 0.0 |
| 0.5 | 5 | 2.5 |
| 1.0 | 5 | 5.0 |
| 1.5 | 5 | 7.5 |
| 2.0 | 5 | 10.0 |

\image(figures/nearest_neighbors_weights.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Exact-match predictions use matching rows; otherwise inverse-distance weights interpolate these two targets.)
