> Checking the theory

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto x = n::tensor({0, 0, 2}, {3, 1}), q = n::tensor({0}, {1, 1});
  const n::ml::TensorNeighbors regression(x, q, 3, true), classification(x, q, 2);
  const auto predicted = regression.regress(n::tensor({2, 4, 20}));
  const auto label = classification.classify(n::from_vector(std::vector<int64_t>{0, 1, 1}), 2);
  if (std::abs(predicted.item() - 3) > 1e-6 || label.item() != 0)
    return 1;
  std::print("Exact-match prediction {}; tied class vote {}; neighbour indices {}.\n",
             predicted.item(), label.item(), classification.indices.to_vector<int64_t>());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Exact-match prediction 3; tied class vote 0; neighbour indices [0, 1].
// result_end

> A dataset example

A fixed k=5 model uses only the petal measurements. The seeded stratified three-fold partition reserves fold zero before inference; this single split does not tune k.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
  const auto names = iris.get_column<std::string>("Species").values();
  std::vector<std::string> levels = names;
  std::sort(levels.begin(), levels.end());
  levels.erase(std::unique(levels.begin(), levels.end()), levels.end());
  std::vector<double> values;
  std::vector<int> ids;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < iris.nrows(); ++i) {
    values.push_back(iris.get_column("Petal.Length").values()[i]);
    values.push_back(iris.get_column("Petal.Width").values()[i]);
    ids.push_back(std::find(levels.begin(), levels.end(), names[i]) - levels.begin());
  }
  const auto x = n::from_vector(values, {150, 2}),
             y = n::from_vector(std::vector<int64_t>(ids.begin(), ids.end()));
  const auto fold = n::ml::StratifiedKFold(ids, 3, 17)[0];
  const std::vector<int64_t> tr(fold.training.begin(), fold.training.end()),
      te(fold.validation.begin(), fold.validation.end());
  const auto tx = n::take_rows(x, tr), test = n::take_rows(x, te);
  const auto mean = n::mean(tx, {0}, true),
             scale = n::sqrt(n::mean(n::square(tx - mean), {0}, true));
  const n::ml::TensorNeighbors neighbors((tx - mean) / scale, (test - mean) / scale, 5);
  const auto prediction = neighbors.classify(n::take_rows(y, tr), 3);
  const double accuracy =
      n::mean(n::cast(n::equal(prediction, n::take_rows(y, te)), n::DType::Float64)).item();
  std::print("{} reference rows, {} held-out rows; tensor-neighbour accuracy {:.6f}.\n",
             tx.size(0), test.size(0), accuracy);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
100 reference rows, 50 held-out rows; tensor-neighbour accuracy 0.960000.
// result_end


> Conclusion

The exact two-point calculation, duplicate policy and class tie rule match the native predictions.
The Iris result is a single fixed held-out evaluation. Choosing k, metric or predictors would
require a validation procedure that also refits scaling inside its training partitions.

> References

- [Cover and Hart (1967), Nearest neighbor pattern classification](https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1967.1053964).
